Chapter: ChanRecvWakeup

 The receiver-side companion to chan-lost-wakeup. A consumer blocks on an
 empty capacity-one channel; a producer sends one value, and the wake path
 hands it across. Both finish and proc 0 -- which waits on both -- prints
 "rdone".

 On a correct compiler this prints "rdone" cooperatively: nothing runs
 between the consumer's emptiness check and its commit to BLOCKED, so the
 wake is never lost. The recv-block lost-wakeup (BACKLOG 4.11g) lives in a
 ~15-instruction window that no timer reliably hits; this test is driven to
 a deterministic hang by a throwaway compiler built with the recv-block
 yield mutant. See docs/Test/Active/YieldInjectionMutant.md.

Section: Body

  consumer : Integer -> [Console, Concurrent] Integer
  consumer (ch) = act
    x <- (chan-kern-recv-block ch)
    x
  end

  producer : Integer -> [Console, Concurrent] Integer
  producer (ch) = act
    a <- (chan-kern-send-block ch 42)
    a
  end

  opening : [Console, Concurrent] Nothing = act
    ch <- chan-kern-create 1
    c <- process-spawn (\z -> consumer ch)
    p <- process-spawn (\z -> producer ch)
    wc <- (process-wait c)
    wp <- (process-wait p)
    print-line-uni "rdone"
  end
